// error
// #4533: `let p: P` was E0083 — `P` has a class-typed field, so it has no zero
// value (§13.4) — while `let w: (i64, P)` for the SAME `P` was accepted and
// lowered to a zeroed `P` whose `q` stayed null. Before #4500 that spelling
// segfaulted at rc=2; after it, it printed `0` at rc=0, which removed the
// crash without settling which verdict the language gives. Owner ruling
// 2026-09-10: the two spellings agree and both refuse, so the checker recurses
// into a tuple's members (`vTupleNoZeroMember`, validatezero.bit) instead of
// lowering narrowing `zeroIsLiveRecord` — that predicate is one answer for
// three lowering sites and narrowing it puts the null back at all three.
//
// A tuple member is NOT judged by the field rule: #4414/#4500 fill a tuple's
// class-typed member with a live zeroed instance, so `let cl: (i64, Q)` for a
// `Q` that HAS a zero value stays legal and is pinned by
// _tests_/cases/run_tuple_zero_binding.bit's `member-class` arm. Only a member
// whose own type has no zero value refuses, which is why `Q` below is bound
// through a tuple two lines above the rejected one and reports nothing.
class Q {
  v: i64,
}

class P {
  q: Q,
}

fn main() {
  let ok: (i64, Q)
  print("${ok.0} ${ok.1.v}\n")
  let w: (i64, P)
  print("${w.1.q.v}\n")
}
